from lib import *
C=10;S='science'
lesson(C,S,'laws-of-motion','newtons-laws-and-momentum',"Newton's laws, momentum and impulse","The sudden stop","When a bus brakes suddenly, you lurch forward. Your body wants to keep moving - that is inertia, the first clue to Newton's laws.",
["Newton's first law: a body stays at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless an external force acts on it. This property is inertia. @ first law and inertia",
 "Momentum p = mass x velocity (p = mv). It is a vector and its unit is kg m/s. @ p = mv",
 "Newton's second law: the rate of change of momentum is directly proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction of the force, which gives F = ma. @ F = ma",
 "Force is measured in newton: 1 N = 1 kg m/s squared. @ newton",
 "Impulse = force x time = change in momentum (Ft = mv - mu). @ impulse",
 "Newton's third law: to every action there is an equal and opposite reaction; the two forces act on different bodies. @ third law",
 "Law of conservation of momentum: if no external force acts, the total momentum before a collision equals the total momentum after. @ conservation of momentum",
 "Recoil of a gun and the launch of a rocket are everyday uses of the third law and of conservation of momentum. @ recoil and rockets"],
("A 2 kg trolley moving at 3 m/s collides and sticks to a 1 kg trolley at rest. Find their common velocity.",[("Momentum before = 2 x 3 + 1 x 0 = 6 kg m/s.","Add the momenta of both bodies."),("After the collision both move together with mass 3 kg and velocity v: 3v.","Total mass is 2 + 1."),("3v = 6, so v = 2 m/s.","Conservation of momentum.")]),
("State Newton's three laws in your own words with one example each.",["Inertia","F = ma","Equal and opposite reaction"],["Thinking action and reaction cancel because they act on one body"]),
["First law: inertia; second law: F = ma; third law: action-reaction.","Momentum = mass x velocity.","Impulse = Ft = change in momentum.","Total momentum is conserved if no external force acts."],
["n|Force (N) needed to accelerate a 5 kg body at 2 m/s squared|10|F = ma = 5 x 2.",
 "n|Momentum (kg m/s) of a 4 kg body moving at 3 m/s|12|p = mv.",
 "t|Action and reaction forces act on the same body.|False|They act on two different bodies.",
 "m|The SI unit of force is|newton|joule~Unit of work;watt~Unit of power|1 N = 1 kg m/s squared.",
 "n|A 0.5 kg ball changes velocity from 0 to 4 m/s. Impulse (N s) is|2|0.5 x 4.",
 "t|Total momentum is conserved in a collision if no external force acts.|True|Law of conservation of momentum.",
 "n|A 60 kg person accelerates at 1.5 m/s squared. Force (N) is|90|60 x 1.5.",
 "s|The tendency of a body to resist a change in its state is called ____.|inertia|First law."])
lesson(C,S,'optics','lens-formula-and-power',"Refraction, lens formula and power of a lens","The magnifying glass","A magnifying glass makes small print big. It is a convex lens that bends (refracts) light to form an enlarged image.",
["Refraction is the bending of light when it passes from one transparent medium to another because its speed changes. @ refraction",
 "Snell's law: sin i / sin r is constant for two media; this constant is the refractive index n = c / v. @ refractive index",
 "A convex lens converges light and has a real principal focus; a concave lens diverges light. @ convex and concave",
 "Lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f, with the sign convention that distances are measured from the optical centre and u is negative for a real object. @ 1/v - 1/u = 1/f",
 "Magnification m = v/u = height of image / height of object. @ m = v/u",
 "Power of a lens P = 1/f with f in metres; the unit is dioptre (D). Convex lenses have positive power and concave lenses negative. @ power in dioptre",
 "Defects of the eye: myopia (short sight) is corrected by a concave lens and hypermetropia (long sight) by a convex lens. @ myopia and hypermetropia"],
("An object is 30 cm in front of a convex lens of focal length 10 cm. Find the image distance.",[("u = -30 cm and f = +10 cm. Use 1/v = 1/f + 1/u.","Apply the sign convention."),("1/v = 1/10 - 1/30 = 3/30 - 1/30 = 2/30 = 1/15.","Common denominator."),("v = +15 cm. The image is real, 15 cm on the other side of the lens.","Positive v means a real image.")]),
("State the lens formula and the sign convention you use.",["1/v - 1/u = 1/f","Distances from optical centre","Power = 1/f in dioptre"],["Forgetting the negative sign of u"]),
["Lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f.","Magnification m = v/u.","Power P = 1/f (dioptre).","Myopia: concave lens; hypermetropia: convex lens."],
["n|Power (D) of a lens of focal length 0.5 m|2|P = 1/0.5.",
 "t|A concave lens has a negative focal length.|True|By the sign convention.",
 "m|Myopia is corrected using a|concave lens|convex lens~That corrects long sight;plane mirror~It does not bend light|Concave lens diverges the rays.",
 "n|A convex lens has f = 20 cm. Its power in dioptres is|5|100/20.",
 "t|Refraction occurs because the speed of light changes between media.|True|That causes bending.",
 "n|Magnification when v = 20 cm and u = -10 cm is (ignoring sign, a number)|2|v/u = 2 in size.",
 "n|Refractive index when light travels at 2 x 10^8 m/s in glass (c = 3 x 10^8) is|1.5|c / v.",
 "s|The unit of power of a lens is ____.|dioptre|Unit D."])
lesson(C,S,'electricity','ohms-law-and-circuits',"Ohm's law, resistances in series and parallel, power","The torch and the cell","A small cell lights a torch bulb. How much current flows, and what happens when we add a second bulb in a row, or beside the first?",
["Electric current I is the rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t; unit ampere (A). Potential difference V is the work done per unit charge, unit volt (V). @ I = Q/t",
 "Ohm's law: at constant temperature the current through a conductor is proportional to the potential difference, so V = IR. @ V = IR",
 "Resistance R is measured in ohm; it depends on length, area and the material: R = rho l / A, where rho is resistivity. @ R = rho l / A",
 "Resistors in series: R = R1 + R2 + R3, and the same current flows through each. @ series",
 "Resistors in parallel: 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3, and the same voltage is across each; the total is smaller than any one. @ parallel",
 "Heating effect: H = I squared R t (Joule's law). @ H = I^2 R t",
 "Electric power P = VI = I squared R = V squared / R, in watts; energy in kWh = power in kW x time in hours. @ P = VI"],
("Resistors of 6 ohm and 3 ohm are in parallel across a 6 V battery. Find the total resistance and current.",[("1/R = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2.","Add reciprocals."),("R = 2 ohm.","Smaller than either resistor."),("I = V/R = 6/2 = 3 A.","Ohm's law.")]),
("State Ohm's law and give the rules for series and parallel resistors.",["V = IR","Series: R adds","Parallel: reciprocals add"],["Adding resistances in parallel directly"]),
["V = IR.","Series R = R1 + R2; parallel 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2.","Power P = VI; H = I^2 R t.","1 kWh = 1 unit of electrical energy."],
["n|Current (A) through 10 ohm with 20 V across it|2|I = V/R.",
 "n|Total resistance (ohm) of 4 ohm and 6 ohm in series|10|4 + 6.",
 "t|Total resistance of parallel resistors is smaller than the smallest one.|True|Adding paths reduces resistance.",
 "n|Power (W) of a bulb using 0.5 A at 220 V|110|P = VI.",
 "n|Total resistance (ohm) of 2 ohm and 2 ohm in parallel|1|1/2 + 1/2 = 1.",
 "t|Ohm's law holds for all conductors at all temperatures.|False|It holds at constant temperature for ohmic conductors.",
 "n|Energy (kWh) used by a 2 kW heater in 3 hours|6|2 x 3.",
 "s|The unit of resistance is the ____.|ohm|Unit symbol is the Greek letter omega."])
lesson(C,S,'atoms-and-molecules','mole-concept',"The mole concept and molar mass","Counting by weighing","A shop does not count rice grain by grain; it weighs. Chemists count atoms the same way. The mole connects the tiny world of atoms to masses we can weigh.",
["One mole of any substance contains 6.022 x 10^23 particles, called Avogadro's number. @ 6.022 x 10^23",
 "Atomic mass is measured in atomic mass units; the gram atomic mass of an element is the mass of one mole of its atoms. @ gram atomic mass",
 "Molar mass is the mass of one mole of a substance in grams; for water H2O it is 2 x 1 + 16 = 18 g. @ molar mass",
 "Number of moles = mass in grams / molar mass. @ n = m / M",
 "Number of particles = number of moles x 6.022 x 10^23. @ particles = n x N",
 "One mole of any gas at STP (standard temperature and pressure) occupies 22.4 litres. @ 22.4 L at STP",
 "Percentage composition = (mass of element / molar mass of compound) x 100. @ percentage composition"],
("Find the number of moles and molecules in 36 g of water.",[("Molar mass of water = 18 g/mol.","2 x 1 + 16."),("Moles = 36/18 = 2.","Mass divided by molar mass."),("Molecules = 2 x 6.022 x 10^23 = 1.2044 x 10^24.","Moles times Avogadro's number.")]),
("What is a mole and how do you convert mass into moles?",["6.022 x 10^23 particles","n = mass / molar mass","22.4 L for gases at STP"],["Confusing atomic mass with molar mass"]),
["1 mole = 6.022 x 10^23 particles.","n = mass / molar mass.","1 mole of gas at STP = 22.4 L.","Molar mass of H2O = 18 g/mol."],
["n|Molar mass of CO2 in g/mol (C = 12, O = 16)|44|12 + 32.",
 "n|Moles in 88 g of CO2|2|88/44.",
 "t|One mole of any gas occupies 22.4 litres at STP.|True|Molar volume at STP.",
 "n|Mass (g) of 0.5 mole of water|9|0.5 x 18.",
 "n|Volume (L) at STP of 2 moles of a gas|44.8|2 x 22.4.",
 "t|Avogadro's number is about 6.022 x 10^23.|True|Particles per mole.",
 "n|Molar mass of NaCl in g/mol (Na = 23, Cl = 35.5)|58.5|23 + 35.5.",
 "s|The number of particles in one mole is called ____'s number.|Avogadro|Named after Amedeo Avogadro."])
lesson(C,S,'solutions','concentration-and-solubility',"Solutions: solubility and concentration","The sugar in tea","Stir a spoon of sugar in tea and it vanishes; stir in ten and some stays at the bottom. A solution can hold only so much.",
["A solution is a homogeneous mixture of a solute dissolved in a solvent. @ solution",
 "A saturated solution holds the maximum solute at a given temperature; an unsaturated one can dissolve more; a supersaturated one holds more than normal. @ saturated",
 "Solubility is the mass of solute that dissolves in 100 g of solvent at a given temperature to make a saturated solution. @ solubility",
 "For most solids, solubility increases with temperature; for gases it decreases. @ temperature effect",
 "Mass percentage = (mass of solute / mass of solution) x 100. @ mass percent",
 "Volume percentage = (volume of solute / volume of solution) x 100. @ volume percent",
 "Hydrated salts keep water of crystallisation, for example CuSO4.5H2O (blue vitriol). @ water of crystallisation"],
("10 g of salt is dissolved in 90 g of water. Find the mass percentage of the solution.",[("Mass of solution = 10 + 90 = 100 g.","Solute plus solvent."),("Percentage = 10/100 x 100.","Use the formula."),("Mass percentage = 10 percent.","Salt is 10 percent of the solution.")]),
("Define solubility and explain how temperature affects it.",["Mass of solute per 100 g solvent","Solids more soluble when hot","Gases less soluble when hot"],["Mixing up solute and solvent"]),
["Solution = solute + solvent.","Saturated solution holds the maximum solute.","Mass percent = solute / solution x 100.","Solubility of solids rises with temperature."],
["n|20 g of sugar in 80 g of water. Mass percent of sugar|20|20/100 x 100.",
 "t|A saturated solution can dissolve more solute at the same temperature.|False|It already holds the maximum.",
 "m|In a salt solution the solvent is|water|salt~That is the solute;both~Only one is the solvent|The dissolving medium.",
 "t|Gases dissolve less in water as temperature rises.|True|Gas solubility drops with temperature.",
 "n|Mass of solute (g) in 200 g of a 5 percent solution|10|5 percent of 200.",
 "m|Solubility is expressed as grams of solute per|100 g of solvent|1 kg of solution~Not the definition;1 litre of air~Not relevant|Standard definition.",
 "t|CuSO4.5H2O is a hydrated salt.|True|It contains water of crystallisation.",
 "s|The substance that dissolves in a solution is the ____.|solute|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,'types-of-chemical-reactions','balancing-and-reaction-types',"Balancing equations and types of chemical reactions","Cooking changes","Frying an egg or burning wood changes the substance for ever. In a chemical reaction atoms are rearranged, never created or destroyed.",
["A chemical equation shows reactants on the left and products on the right; the arrow means 'gives'. @ chemical equation",
 "By the law of conservation of mass, each element must have the same number of atoms on both sides, so equations are balanced with coefficients. @ balanced equation",
 "Combination: A + B gives AB, for example 2Mg + O2 gives 2MgO. @ combination",
 "Decomposition: AB gives A + B, for example 2H2O gives 2H2 + O2 on electrolysis. @ decomposition",
 "Displacement: a more reactive metal replaces a less reactive one, for example Zn + CuSO4 gives ZnSO4 + Cu. @ displacement",
 "Double displacement: two compounds exchange ions, for example NaCl + AgNO3 gives AgCl (white precipitate) + NaNO3. @ double displacement",
 "Reactions are exothermic if they release heat and endothermic if they absorb heat; oxidation is gain of oxygen (or loss of electrons) and reduction the opposite. @ exothermic and endothermic"],
("Balance: H2 + O2 gives H2O.",[("Count atoms: left has 2 H and 2 O; right has 2 H and 1 O.","Oxygen is unbalanced."),("Put 2 before H2O: H2 + O2 gives 2H2O. Now O is 2 = 2 but H is 2 on the left and 4 on the right.","Fix hydrogen next."),("Put 2 before H2: 2H2 + O2 gives 2H2O. Both sides have 4 H and 2 O.","Balanced.")]),
("Name four types of chemical reaction with one example each.",["Combination, decomposition","Displacement, double displacement","Law of conservation of mass"],["Changing subscripts instead of coefficients"]),
["Balance equations with coefficients, not subscripts.","Types: combination, decomposition, displacement, double displacement.","Exothermic releases heat; endothermic absorbs heat.","Mass is conserved in every reaction."],
["m|2Mg + O2 gives 2MgO is a|combination reaction|decomposition reaction~Two substances combine;displacement reaction~Nothing is replaced|Two reactants make one product.",
 "t|In a balanced equation the atoms on both sides are equal.|True|Law of conservation of mass.",
 "m|Zn + CuSO4 gives ZnSO4 + Cu is a|displacement reaction|combination reaction~Not a single product;decomposition reaction~Not a single reactant|Zn replaces Cu.",
 "n|Coefficient of H2O in 2H2 + O2 gives ?H2O is|2|Balance the hydrogen atoms.",
 "t|Endothermic reactions release heat.|False|They absorb heat.",
 "t|Silver chloride is a white precipitate.|True|Formed when NaCl meets AgNO3.",
 "n|In 2Mg + O2 gives 2MgO the number of Mg atoms on the left is|2|The coefficient is 2.",
 "s|A reaction that releases heat is ____.|exothermic|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,'carbon-and-its-compounds','hydrocarbons-and-functional-groups',"Hydrocarbons, nomenclature and functional groups","The kitchen gas","The LPG in your kitchen is a hydrocarbon - a compound of only carbon and hydrogen. Carbon forms millions of compounds because it bonds with itself in chains and rings.",
["Carbon has four valence electrons and forms four covalent bonds; it can bond to other carbon atoms (catenation). @ catenation",
 "Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons CnH2n+2 with single bonds: methane CH4, ethane C2H6, propane C3H8. @ alkanes",
 "Alkenes have a double bond CnH2n (ethene C2H4); alkynes have a triple bond CnH2n-2 (ethyne C2H2). @ alkenes and alkynes",
 "Isomers are compounds with the same molecular formula but different structures, such as butane and isobutane. @ isomers",
 "A functional group decides the chemical properties: alcohol -OH, aldehyde -CHO, ketone C=O, carboxylic acid -COOH. @ functional group",
 "IUPAC names come from the number of carbons (meth-, eth-, prop-, but-) plus the ending: -ane, -ene, -yne, -ol, -al, -one, -oic acid. @ IUPAC naming",
 "Ethanol is used as a solvent and fuel; ethanoic acid (vinegar) reacts with ethanol to form an ester with a fruity smell. @ ethanol and ethanoic acid"],
("Name C3H8 and write the general formula it follows, and name CH3CH2OH.",[("C3H8 has 3 carbons and only single bonds: propane.","Prefix prop- and ending -ane."),("It fits CnH2n+2 with n = 3: C3H8.","2 x 3 + 2 = 8."),("CH3CH2OH has two carbons and an -OH group: ethanol.","eth- plus -ol.")]),
("Why does carbon form so many compounds?",["Tetravalency","Catenation","Different functional groups"],["Thinking carbon only forms single bonds"]),
["Alkanes CnH2n+2, alkenes CnH2n, alkynes CnH2n-2.","Catenation: carbon bonds to carbon.","Functional groups: -OH, -CHO, C=O, -COOH.","Isomers share a formula but differ in structure."],
["n|Number of hydrogen atoms in an alkane with 4 carbons (C4H?)|10|2 x 4 + 2.",
 "m|The general formula of alkenes is|CnH2n|CnH2n+2~That is alkanes;CnH2n-2~That is alkynes|One double bond.",
 "t|Carbon is tetravalent.|True|It forms four bonds.",
 "n|Number of carbons in propane|3|prop- means three.",
 "t|Methane contains a double bond.|False|It has only single bonds.",
 "m|The functional group of an alcohol is|-OH|-COOH~That is carboxylic acid;-CHO~That is aldehyde|Hydroxyl group.",
 "t|Isomers have the same molecular formula.|True|But different structures.",
 "s|The ability of carbon to bond with itself is called ____.|catenation|Definition."])
